= Practice Questions — Part 2 (Q6 to Q10)

#mcq(6, [During the secondary (biological) stage of sewage treatment in an aeration tank, what biological structure is formed to consume dissolved organic matter?], (
  [Flocs, composed of masses of aerobic bacteria associated with fungal filaments to form mesh-like networks],
  [Primary sludge, composed of gravitational sediments of pebbles and floating plastics],
  [Anaerobic biofilms, composed of pure cultures of methanogenic archaebacteria],
  [Mycorrhizal root nodules, which absorb dissolved nitrogenous phosphates directly from effluent],
))
#solution(6, [In secondary sewage treatment aeration tanks, continuous air supply and agitation foster the growth of *flocs*, which are strictly defined in NCERT as *masses of bacteria associated with fungal filaments to form mesh-like structures*. As these aerobic flocs metabolize, they consume the dissolved organic matter in the effluent, dramatically lowering its Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD).], answer: [A])

#mcq(7, [Biogas is a combustible fuel produced in anaerobic sludge digesters and rural gobar gas plants. Which microbial group and primary gas constituent correctly describe biogas?], (
  [Aerobic nitrifying bacteria producing sulfur dioxide ($S O_2$) as the major combustible component],
  [Anaerobic methanogenic bacteria producing methane ($C H_4$) as the major combustible component],
  [Photosynthetic cyanobacteria producing ozone ($O_3$) as the major combustible fuel component],
  [Facultative heterotrophic yeasts producing pure carbon monoxide ($C O$) as the primary constituent],
))
#solution(7, [Methanogens, such as *_Methanobacterium_*, are obligate anaerobes that grow *strictly in the absence of molecular oxygen*. In anaerobic sludge digesters and cattle dung plants, they digest cellulosic organic materials to produce *Biogas*, which contains $50% - 70%$ *methane ($C H_4$)* as its primary combustible constituent, along with $30% - 40% C O_2$ and trace quantities of $H_2 S$ and $H_2$.], answer: [B])

#mcq(8, [A farmer wishes to protect crop roots against soil-borne fungal pathogens without applying synthetic chemical fungicides. Which biocontrol fungus should be introduced into the root rhizosphere?], (
  [_Penicillium notatum_],
  [_Aspergillus niger_],
  [_Trichoderma_ species],
  [_Monascus purpureus_],
))
#solution(8, [*_Trichoderma_* is a genus of free-living, soil-dwelling filamentous fungi that thrive in the root ecosystems (rhizosphere). They are highly effective *biocontrol agents* against several fungal plant root pathogens, suppressing disease through competitive rhizosphere colonization, mycoparasitism, and the secretion of antimicrobial hydrolytic enzymes.], answer: [C])

#mcq(9, [Root nodules of leguminous plants host symbiotic bacteria capable of converting atmospheric nitrogen into bioavailable ammonia. This vital biofertiliser bacterium is:], (
  [_Azotobacter_],
  [_Azospirillum_],
  [_Oscillatoria_],
  [_Rhizobium_],
))
#solution(9, [The Gram-negative bacterium *_Rhizobium_* establishes a specific symbiotic association with the roots of leguminous plants (pulses, beans, peas), developing specialized root nodules. Inside these anaerobic nodules, the bacterial enzyme nitrogenase reduces atmospheric dinitrogen ($N_2$) into ammonia, supplying vital organic nitrogen to the host plant while receiving carbohydrates.], answer: [D])

#mcq(10, [A patient is rushed to the hospital emergency department exhibiting acute myocardial infarction caused by an occluding coronary thrombus. The emergency physician should immediately administer which recombinant enzyme as a "clot buster"?], (
  [Lipase],
  [Streptokinase],
  [Pectinase],
  [Protease],
))
#solution(10, [*Streptokinase* is an enzyme produced by the bacterium *_Streptococcus_* and subsequently modified by genetic engineering. It functions as a potent *clot buster*, activating plasminogen to dissolve intravascular fibrin clots (thrombi) that occlude coronary blood vessels, thereby restoring myocardial blood perfusion in acute heart attack patients.], answer: [B])

